    38	## 2. Trust invariants
    39	
    40	1. Untrusted source is any fork pull request, pull-request head, arbitrary
    41	   branch, mutable tag, downloaded artifact without a verified digest, or
    42	   rerun whose candidate identity differs from its original attempt.
    43	2. Repository source becomes release input only as an exact commit reachable
    44	   from the protected default branch and bound into an immutable candidate
    45	   manifest. A tag is a label, never identity authority.
    46	3. Workflow code that accepts a release candidate or can reach production
    47	   credentials is loaded only from the protected default branch. A tag event is
    48	   handled first by an unprivileged intake and cannot accept its own candidate.
    49	   The trusted `workflow_run` entry point independently resolves repository
    50	   metadata and never executes or consumes intake code, checkout, caches,
    51	   artifacts, outputs, or verdicts. `pull_request_target` is prohibited for
    52	   building, executing, caching, or uploading pull-request code.
    53	4. Workflow and job permissions default to `contents: read`; every other
    54	   `GITHUB_TOKEN` permission is `none`. A job elevates one named permission only
    55	   for its own call site and only after protected-environment approval.
    56	   Repository Actions settings enforce the read-only default so a workflow from
    57	   a tag cannot self-elevate. No production secret exists at repository or
    58	   organization scope; protected environments restrict deployment refs to the
    59	   protected default branch and reject tag/non-default workflow jobs before any
    60	   secret-bearing step.
    61	5. Pull-request jobs have no production environment and receive no certificate,
    62	   private key, certificate password, App Store Connect issuer/key, provisioning
    63	   profile secret, notarization credential, Sparkle private key, feed-origin
    64	   credential, publication token, or privileged runner.
    65	6. Every third-party action is pinned to a reviewed full commit SHA. A mutable
    66	   tag, branch, range, or unreviewed action source fails before execution.
    67	7. Pull-request caches and artifacts are untrusted accelerators. They are never
    68	   release inputs; cache hits are revalidated against source and manifests.
    69	8. Release jobs consume one candidate manifest and exact digests. Reruns may
    70	   reuse that identity but may not resolve a new tag, branch head, dependency,
    71	   cache namespace, or artifact under the old release attempt.
    72	9. Production publication is serialized and feed-last. Cancellation or any
    73	   failed/missing/unknown prerequisite leaves the candidate non-promotable.
    74	10. Absence and read failure are distinct. A missing optional row is `deferred`
    75	    or `not-applicable`; an unreadable or indeterminate required input is
    76	    `unknown` and fails closed.
