   174	### 3.3 Release intake and immutable candidate preparation
   175	
   176	`release-intake.yml` triggers on a `v*` tag push. It is unprivileged and may be
   177	loaded from the tagged commit, so it is never a policy authority. Its sole job,
   178	`release/request-intake`, uses `contents: read`, no environment, secrets,
   179	cache, artifact upload, checkout, executable candidate code, publication
   180	permission, or production network. The run record itself is diagnostic evidence
   181	retained for 14 days. Success or failure merely completes an intake attempt and
   182	cannot accept a candidate.
   183	
   184	| Job / non-blocking intake check | Actor trust and input | Token / environment / secrets | Network | Outputs / retention | Approval |
   185	| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   186	| `release/request-intake` | Untrusted tagged workflow revision and tag event identity; never policy or candidate authority | Repository-enforced `contents: read`; no environment; no repository/organization production secret | None | GitHub run/event metadata only; diagnostic/14d; no cache or uploaded artifact | None |
   187	
   188	The intake boundary is enforced outside tagged YAML by repository Actions
   189	settings (`GITHUB_TOKEN` read-only), absence of repository/organization
   190	production secrets, and protected-environment deployment-branch rules that
   191	admit only the protected default branch. A tagged workflow that changes its own
   192	permissions, steps, name, or environment reference therefore gains no release
   193	authority; disappearance or mutation of the expected intake produces no trusted
   194	candidate.
   195	
   196	`release-prepare.yml` triggers only on `workflow_run: completed` for the named
   197	`Release request intake` workflow. GitHub binds this run and its workflow file
   198	to the protected default branch. There is no selectable-ref
   199	`workflow_dispatch` and no direct tag/push trigger on this trusted workflow.
   200	The first job independently reads the triggering run and repository ref metadata
   201	through the GitHub API, then requires one exact `v*` tag, an exact commit
   202	reachable from the protected default branch, the expected event/repository, a
   203	readable intake conclusion, `github.ref` equal to the default branch, and
   204	`github.workflow_sha` equal to the protected default-branch workflow revision
   205	whose SHA-256 policy digest is checked into the release-policy manifest. Intake
   206	artifacts, caches, output strings, checkout, logs, code, and asserted conclusions
   207	are never downloaded or executed. Missing, ambiguous, or inconsistent metadata
   208	is `unknown` and stops before candidate acceptance.
   209	
   210	Every credential-capable reusable workflow is called as
   211	`owner/repository/.github/workflows/<file>.yml@<full-reviewed-commit-SHA>` and
   212	that SHA plus file digest appears in the immutable candidate. Relative local
   213	workflow calls and tag/branch refs are prohibited for release authority because
   214	they resolve from mutable or caller-selected code.
