     1	# Platform and distribution specification
     2	
     3	## Project shape
     4	
     5	The current SwiftPM executable is insufficient for embedded Network Extension
     6	targets and iOS distribution. The target project is a generated Xcode workspace
     7	using Tuist 4.202.5, pinned exactly by repository-local Mise. Generation and CI
     8	select an exact Xcode version and apply the deterministic comparison policy in
     9	TASK-260715-3r0993; they do not consume an unversioned global Tuist installation.
    10	
    11	Planned targets:
    12	
    13	- `ReluxProxyMac` containing app;
    14	- `ReluxProxyMacTunnel` packet-tunnel system extension for direct Developer ID
    15	  distribution;
    16	- `ReluxProxyIOS` containing app;
    17	- `ReluxProxyIOSTunnel` packet tunnel extension;
    18	- `ReluxTunnelCore` Swift package shared by both providers;
    19	- `ReluxTunnelHarness` macOS CLI for fast packet/SSH/relay experiments;
    20	- `relux-relay` portable remote executable and protocol test target.
    21	
    22	The existing menu bar SOCKS app remains buildable until an explicit migration
    23	task replaces it. Migration MUST preserve or deliberately retire its profile
    24	defaults and release history.
    25	
    26	## Apple capabilities
    27	
    28	Both containing apps manage custom VPN configurations through
    29	`NETunnelProviderManager`. Host and extension App IDs require the Network
    30	Extension packet-tunnel entitlement and matching provisioning profiles. App
    31	Groups and Keychain Sharing provide the minimum app/extension state boundary.
    32	
    33	**Gate P0:** confirm Relux Works organization enrollment, bundle IDs, Network
    34	Extension capability/profiles, and device installation before packet-plane work
    35	is considered viable.
    36	
    37	The app must be primarily a networking product and accurately disclose its
    38	extension and VPN behavior. Apple App Review Guideline 5.4 requires VPN apps to
    39	be offered by an organization and imposes privacy and regional-law obligations.
    40	
    41	## Minimum platform policy
    42	
    43	New generated Apple targets use iOS 18.0 and macOS 15.0. The shipped legacy
    44	`ReluxProxy` SwiftPM product remains a separate macOS 14.0 compatibility lane
    45	until its migration/retirement decision says otherwise. The baseline MUST
    46	support:
    47	
    48	- a current iOS release on a physical iPhone used for memory/lifecycle gates —
    49	  **deferred for the macOS-first goal (ADR-024)**: while iOS is deferred this row
    50	  is a named deferred gap, never a pass, never inferred from Mac results, and it
    51	  is re-armed unchanged and becomes mandatory again before any iOS release;
    52	- a current macOS release on Apple silicon — this is the **only** physical
    53	  baseline that gates the macOS prototype goal;
    54	- the oldest iOS/macOS versions for which all selected Network Extension,
    55	  Network.framework, Swift concurrency, signing, and dependency requirements can
    56	  be tested in CI.
    57	
    58	The dated evidence, named physical devices, minimum/current CI matrix, Xcode
    59	pins, and upgrade ownership are recorded in
    60	`.research/260720_task-260715-3r0993-project-generator-deployment-target-policy.md`.
    61	Any later dependency that requires a higher target blocks generation until this
    62	policy and ADR-016 are reviewed; it does not silently rewrite the manifest.
    63	
    64	No deployment target is lowered by adding private API or fragile compatibility
    65	shims.
    66	
    67	## Signing and release channels
    68	
    69	### macOS
    70	
    71	Developer ID distribution uses hardened runtime, nested signing in inside-out
    72	order, notarization, stapling, Gatekeeper validation, checksums, and provenance
    73	attestation. The packet-tunnel system extension and containing app need
    74	compatible entitlements/profiles and the same approved Team ID. A stable
    75	`ReluxProxy.dmg` release asset may continue, but on a private repository it
    76	requires authenticated GitHub access.
    77	
    78	### iOS
    79	
    80	iOS uses Apple Development for device spikes and Apple Distribution through
    81	TestFlight/App Store. Developer ID and notarization do not apply to iOS.
    82	TestFlight is required for lifecycle, entitlement, and review-preparation
    83	iterations before App Store submission.
    84	
    85	### macOS self-update
    86	
    87	The macOS app ships the ADR-018 in-app update design so security fixes can reach
    88	users without a manual re-download. This remains accepted design until the
    89	integration, release pipeline, key ceremony, and physical gates pass.
    90	
    91	#### Dependency and integration
    92	
    93	- Pin official Sparkle **2.9.4** exactly through Swift Package Manager at tag
    94	  commit `b6496a74a087257ef5e6da1c5b29a447a60f5bd7`; the official binary-target
    95	  checksum is
    96	  `cb6fdbdc8884f15d62a616e79face92b08322410fd2d425edc6596ccbf4ba3b0`.
    97	  Ranges, branches, prereleases, and automatic dependency upgrades are
    98	  prohibited. Ship the tagged complete `LICENSE`, including bundled licenses.
    99	- Link and embed Sparkle only in the sandboxed, hardened-runtime
   100	  `ReluxProxyMac` host. Use a host-owned `SPUStandardUpdaterController`; never
   101	  link Sparkle into the packet-tunnel system extension, shared core, harness, or
   102	  iOS.
   103	- The sandboxed host enables `SUEnableInstallerLauncherService` and Sparkle's
   104	  documented `<host-bundle-id>-spks` / `<host-bundle-id>-spki` Mach lookup
   105	  exceptions. Because the host has outbound network-client access, Downloader,
   106	  Installer Connection, and Installer Status XPC services stay disabled.
   107	  Xcode Archive + Developer ID Export must re-sign the framework, both bundled
   108	  XPC services, `Autoupdate`, and `Updater.app`; “Code Sign on Copy” alone is not
   109	  release evidence.
   110	- Hardened Runtime remains enabled on all executable code. Distribution has no
   111	  `get-task-allow`, disabled library validation, unsigned/JIT executable-memory,
   112	  or unreviewed exception entitlement.
   113	
   114	#### Feed and updater settings
   115	
   116	- `SUFeedURL` is
   117	  `https://updates.relux.works/macos/appcast.xml`. The origin is public,
   118	  read-only, ATS-compatible HTTPS; no repository credential or user secret is
   119	  embedded in the app. Private GitHub releases remain the authenticated manual
   120	  download channel and may mirror the exact same final DMG bytes.
   121	- Pin the later ceremony's public key in `SUPublicEDKey`. Set
   122	  `SUVerifyUpdateBeforeExtraction=YES`, `SURequireSignedFeed=YES`, and
   123	  `SUSignedFeedFailureExpirationInterval=0`. Feed, release-note, payload, TLS,
   124	  parse, version, or download failure has no unsigned, alternate-feed, website,
   125	  or expiration fallback.
   126	- Leave `SUEnableAutomaticChecks` absent so Sparkle asks on second launch; set a
   127	  86,400-second schedule, `SUAutomaticallyUpdate=NO`,
   128	  `SUAllowsAutomaticUpdates=NO`, `SUEnableSystemProfiling=NO`, and
   129	  `SUEnableJavaScript=NO`. A user may check manually and a declined automatic
   130	  check preference is respected.
   131	- Stable appcast items omit `sparkle:channel`. Opt-in prereleases use exactly
   132	  `prerelease`; prerelease clients also see Sparkle's unexcludable default
   133	  channel. One monotonically increasing integer `CFBundleVersion` sequence spans
   134	  both channels. Appcast version strings match the app exactly and the baseline
   135	  `sparkle:minimumSystemVersion` is `15.0.0`.
   136	- Appcast and detached release notes use `Cache-Control: no-store`; versioned
   137	  payloads use `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`. Enclosure
   138	  URLs are immutable, same-origin, and non-redirecting. Publish verified assets
   139	  first and atomically replace the signed appcast last.
   140	
   141	#### Signed, already-notarized payload invariant
   142	
   143	The baseline enclosure is a DMG, not ZIP, PKG, or delta. Build and sign all
   144	nested code inside-out, verify exact identities/entitlements and Hardened
   145	Runtime, notarize and staple the app, assemble/sign/notarize/staple the final
   146	DMG, and only then run Sparkle 2.9.4 signing tools over the final immutable DMG
   147	bytes. Any later mutation invalidates publication and requires rebuilding the
   148	Apple and Sparkle evidence. A missing signing input, failed notarization/stapling,
   149	identity or entitlement drift, signature failure, wrong version/channel/floor,
   150	remote digest/header mismatch, or premature feed publication fails CI closed.
   151	
   152	#### System-extension lifecycle
   153	
   154	Apple TN3134 requires a system extension for a directly distributed macOS
   155	packet-tunnel provider. The host must confirm an orderly tunnel stop before
   156	allowing update installation. Sparkle then replaces/relaunches only the
   157	containing app. On relaunch the host separately requests activation/replacement
   158	of the embedded same-Team-ID, same-bundle-ID system extension and does not claim
   159	the new provider active until the OS reports success. The baseline does not
   160	auto-reconnect after update.
   161	
   162	Apple documents that new-version activation can require user approval and can
   163	return restart-required. Exact approval reuse, UI, timing, active-provider, and
   164	managed-system behavior are OS-controlled and remain unknown. Physical clean
   165	install and old-to-new update evidence, including denial, restart, relaunch,
   166	provider-version proof, and manual reconnect, is mandatory under
   167	`TASK-260715-1r48pc` and `TASK-260715-2aessv`.
   168	
   169	#### Withdrawal, rollback, keys, and privacy
   170	
   171	- Withdrawal republishes a valid signed feed without the affected item and
   172	  removes/denies its asset. It prevents new discovery after a fresh check but
   173	  cannot recall already downloaded or installed software.
   174	- Sparkle 2 does not support downgrades. Emergency rollback publishes
   175	  last-known-good source as a newly signed/notarized release with a higher
   176	  `CFBundleVersion`; true downgrade is a separate manual recovery path. There is
   177	  no rollback channel.
   178	- `TASK-260717-ziprhs` owns the human EdDSA key ceremony. The private key exists
   179	  only in a protected release environment plus separately controlled encrypted
   180	  escrow; two custodians approve import, use, rotation, and destruction. No
   181	  secret enters source, PR jobs, logs, artifacts, the feed origin, or board
   182	  resources.
   183	- Planned EdDSA rotation uses an old-key-signed Developer ID DMG bridge that pins
   184	  the new public key while the Developer ID identity stays unchanged. Developer
   185	  ID and EdDSA identities never rotate in the same update. There is no remote
   186	  revocation of a key pinned in installed apps; unsupported recovery remains a
   187	  manual notarized download.
   188	- Update requests carry no Sparkle system profile, custom identifier parameters,
   189	  traffic/destination/DNS data, analytics, JavaScript, or tracking content. The
   190	  origin may keep access-controlled security/availability logs for at most seven
   191	  days, never join them to product profiles, and must be disclosed before
   192	  production.
   193	
   194	The dated authority, sources, human release gates, and downstream contracts are
   195	in [`.research/260721_macos-self-update.md`](../.research/260721_macos-self-update.md).
   196	
   197	## CI/CD contract
   198	
   199	Pull requests run documentation/board validation plus builds and tests available
   200	without production credentials. Release workflows use GitHub Environments and
   201	least-privilege secrets; no certificate, private key, issuer credential, or
   202	provisioning secret is committed.
   203	
   204	Required pipelines:
   205	
   206	1. shared-core unit and protocol conformance tests on macOS;
   207	2. macOS host/system-extension build, entitlement inspection, nested signing,
   208	   notarization/stapling, final-DMG verification, Sparkle 2.9.4 payload/feed
   209	   signing, asset-first/feed-last publication, cache/privacy probes, checksums,
   210	   and stable manual-download asset;
   211	3. iOS host/extension archive, entitlement/provisioning inspection, and
   212	   TestFlight upload;
   213	4. reproducible relay cross-build matrix, tests, checksums, manifest generation,
   214	   and bundling into Apple targets;
   215	5. dependency license/SBOM and secret scanning;
   216	6. semantic version/tag validation and release notes.
   217	
   218	Production release jobs are serialized. Build artifacts retain exact source
   219	commit, dependency lockfiles, relay manifest, protocol version, bundle versions,
   220	and signing identity metadata.
