    38	
    39	## Packet-plane matrix
    40	
    41	- MTU: 1500, 4096, 8500;
    42	- address family: IPv4, native IPv6, dual stack, IPv6-only/NAT64;
    43	- socket pressure: normal, constrained buffers, deliberate receiver stall;
    44	- traffic: short web flows, large download/upload, many idle connections, DNS,
    45	  datagram bursts, and mixed bidirectional load;
    46	- lifecycle: start/stop loops, cancellation during start, UI termination, provider
    47	  stop reason, sleep/wake, and memory warning/pressure;
    48	- scale: staged 100, 250, 500, and configuration-limit concurrent flows, subject
    49	  to the measured device budget rather than an unconditional maximum.
    50	
    51	Pass conditions include no descriptor/task growth across repeated cycles, no
    52	unbounded queue, correct reason-specific drops under induced saturation, and no
    53	ordinary loss under a non-saturated nominal run.
    54	
    55	## SSH matrix
    56	
    57	- target servers: current OpenSSH Linux/macOS plus documented older algorithm
    58	  profiles supported by product scope;
    59	- keys/host keys: Ed25519 and approved fallbacks;
    60	- channels: control, small interactive-like flows, bulk, early close, half-close,
    61	  server reset, and hundreds of concurrent opens;
    62	- loss simulation: representative Wi-Fi latency/loss and lane comparison at
    63	  one, two, and four SSH connections;
    64	- window profiles: 32 KiB, 64 KiB, and capped BDP bulk windows;
    65	- rekey: at least 5 GiB mixed traffic, explicit client threshold, server-initiated
    66	  request, simultaneous active direct-tcpip and relay traffic;
    67	- reconnect: lane-local failure, control-lane failure, host unreachable, auth
    68	  rejection, and host-key change.
    69	
    70	## Relay and protocol tests
    71	
    72	- golden encode/decode vectors for every message/address type;
    73	- incremental and coalesced stream reads;
    74	- malformed/oversized lengths, unknown types/flags, association reuse, idle
    75	  expiry, queue pressure, DNS priority, and process exit;
    76	- Linux/macOS x86_64/arm64 build and smoke execution;
    77	- upload interruption, checksum mismatch, noexec/read-only home, missing hash
    78	  tools, incompatible version, and atomic upgrade;
    79	- degraded TCP + DNS-over-TCP behavior when relay is unavailable.
    80	
    81	## Routing and leak tests
    82	
    83	Automated or repeatable captures verify:
    84	
    85	- SSH connects before default routes and remains outside its own tunnel;
    86	- only documented endpoint/system exceptions use the physical path;
    87	- DNS never falls back to a physical resolver in connected/reasserting states;
    88	- external IPv4/IPv6 and DNS observations match the exit path;
    89	- Wi-Fi to cellular, cellular to Wi-Fi, IPv4 to NAT64, captive network,
    90	  sleep/wake, and server-address changes rebuild state without deadlock;
    91	- compatible and fail-closed modes match their disclosures.
    92	
    93	## Memory and performance
    94	
    95	The 25–30 MiB steady-state target is measured, not asserted. Report physical
    96	footprint, peak, `os_proc_available_memory()`, HEV sessions, channel windows,
    97	queued bytes, lane count, and reconnect overlap.
    98	
    99	The first baseline establishes throughput, time-to-first-byte, DNS latency,
   100	energy, CPU, packet rate, and syscall rate. Later optimizations require a before
   101	and after measurement. A HEV fork is justified only by a demonstrated material
   102	bottleneck and validated regression coverage.
   103	
   104	## Security and release validation
   105	
   106	- host-key first-use/change cases and Keychain access boundaries;
   107	- malformed packet/SOCKS/SSH/relay fuzz targets with bounded allocations;
   108	- log/support-export redaction tests;
   109	- dependency licenses, notices, SBOM, pinned revisions, and relay hash manifest;
   110	- exact entitlements and nested code signatures in archived apps;
   111	- macOS notarization/stapling/Gatekeeper install; iOS/TestFlight install is
   112	  **deferred with iOS (ADR-024)** and is not a gate for the macOS-first goal;
   113	- privacy disclosure checked against actual behavior; the App Review notes check
   114	  is **deferred with the App Store branch (ADR-013)** and re-arms unchanged
   115	  before any submission.
   150	
   151	A local process may probe the loopback SOCKS listener, read weakly protected
   152	configuration, scrape logs, or race the containing app/provider. The implemented
   153	SOCKS boundary binds to loopback, generates per-run credentials, bounds pending
   154	authentication, and does not expose a supported public-proxy configuration.
   155	It is not equivalent to a process-private socket; compromise of the process,
   156	credential memory, or a sufficiently privileged local account remains in scope
   157	as residual risk.
   158	
   159	### F. Lost, stolen, or compromised device
   160	
   161	The accepted design keeps private keys and passphrases in the Data Protection
   162	Keychain and non-secret configuration in the App Group. That storage path is
   163	not implemented yet. When implemented, it still inherits device-unlock,
   164	Keychain-access-group, backup, malware, and OS-compromise risks. No claim covers
   165	a compromised Apple device.
   166	
   167	### G. Supply-chain, CI, maintainer, and update attacker
   168	
   169	An attacker may alter dependencies, build tools, relay assets, application
   170	archives, signing jobs, the appcast, or release metadata. The repository pins
   171	and verifies native dependencies and a credential-isolated relay toolchain, and
   172	CI exercises relay builds. Relay embedding, application SBOM/secret gates,
   173	Developer ID/notarization jobs, signed appcast generation, rollback controls,
   174	and key-rotation drills remain planned. ADR-018 is accepted design intent, not
   175	evidence that self-update is available.
   176	
   177	### H. Relux Works insider or future service operator
   178	
   179	The baseline architecture has no Relux-operated exit, resolver, account, or
   180	traffic telemetry service. Typed runtime diagnostics contain finite aggregate
   181	fields rather than payload, destination, or DNS-name fields. This does not mean
   182	Relux Works can never receive data: distribution infrastructure, a future
   183	opt-in support export, a vulnerability report, or ordinary visits to a Relux
   184	service are separate flows. Any vendor traffic service or telemetry requires a
   185	new threat-model revision and disclosure before implementation.

